-- title:
--   j(q^{p^2}) lies outside ℚ(j,j(qᵖ),j(q^s)_{s∈ S})
-- statement:
--   Work inside the field $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ of Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$, i.e. `LaurentSeries ℚ`. Here `jq` denotes the Laurent series $q^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{jNumQ}$, the $q$-expansion of the modular $j$-invariant obtained from the integral power series `jNum` by base change along $\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Q}$, and for $N\ge 1$ the series `jqN N` is the image of `jq` under `qExpand ℚ N`, the ring endomorphism of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ that multiplies all exponents by $N$; thus `jqN N` is $j(q^{N})$. Let $S$ be a finite set of natural numbers all of whose elements are prime, and let $p$ be a prime with $p \notin S$. The assertion is that $j(q^{p^{2}})$, written as `jqN (p * p)`, does not belong to the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ generated by the set consisting of `jq`, of `jqN p`, and of all series of the form `jqN s` for nonzero $s \in S$. Equivalently, $j(q^{p^{2}})$ is not a rational function over $\mathbb{Q}$ of $j(q)$, $j(q^{p})$ and the $j(q^{s})$ with $s \in S$.
--
--   This is the descent step at level $p^{2}$ in a purely algebraic computation of the degree $[\mathbb{Q}(j)(j_N):\mathbb{Q}(j)] = \psi(N)$, i.e. of the irreducibility of the modular polynomial $\Phi_N$ over $\mathbb{Q}(j)$, for the $q$-expansion model of the function field of $X_0(N)$; it complements the squarefree case [`ModularCurve.jqN_prime_not_mem_adjoin`](thm.html#ModularCurve.jqN_prime_not_mem_adjoin). It is used by [`ModularCurve.exists_isFrickeAutFull_sq`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_isFrickeAutFull_sq) and [`ModularCurve.relfinrank_full_sq`](thm.html#ModularCurve.relfinrank_full_sq).
